  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Vaccines are biological preparations that provide active acquired immunity against infectious diseases by teaching the immune system to recognize and fight pathogens. They are rigorously tested for safety and effectiveness, protecting against diseases like influenza, COVID-19, measles, and HPV. Vaccinations are recommended across all lifespans, available at doctor's offices and pharmacies. How Vaccines Work Imitation: Vaccines mimic infections to teach the immune system to build defense mechanisms without causing the disease itself. Types: Technologies include live-attenuated (weakened germs), inactivated (killed germs), subunits (pieces of germs), mRNA (instructions to make protein), and viral vector vaccines. Key Vaccine Types and Protection COVID-19: Updated vaccines are available, with specific formulations recommended for adults aged 65+ and those at higher risk. Flu: Annual vaccines are recommended, with high-dose options often recommended for older adults to provide greater protection. Routine Immunizations: Essential vaccines for children and adults include diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis (whooping cough), polio, and MMR (measles, mumps, rubella). Disease Prevention: Vaccines prevent many illnesses, including shingles, HPV (cervical cancer), hepatitis B (liver cancer), and pneumococcal disease. Vaccine Safety and Access Safety Monitoring: Vaccines are monitored closely even after approval to ensure safety. Access: Vaccines are widely available at pharmacies, doctor's offices, and local health departments. Cost: In the U.S., the Vaccines for Children program provides vaccines at no cost for eligible children. It is important to keep vaccinations up-to-date to avoid serious illness, particularly when diseases are spreading.For personalized schedules, consult the official CDC vaccine recommendations for infants/children and adults. infants/children adults",
